G(a) \\) into \\( P \\):**\n   \\[\n   P(G(a)) = P(4 - 3a)\n   \\]\n   The function \\( P \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = 4 + 2\\sqrt{x + 2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   P(4 - 3a) = 4 + 2\\sqrt{(4 - 3a) + 2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the expression inside the square root:**\n   \\[\n   P(4 - 3a) = 4 + 2\\sqrt{6 - 3a}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Ensure the argument of the square root is non-negative:**\n   \\[\n   6 - 3a \\geq 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve the inequality for \\( a \\):**\n   \\[\n   6 - 3a \\geq 0\n   \\]\n   Subtract 6 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   -3a \\geq -6\n   \\]\n   Divide both sides by -3 (and reverse the inequality sign):\n   \\[\n   a \\leq 2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Determine the largest constant \\( a \\):**\n   The largest value of \\( a \\) that satisfies the inequality \\( a \\leq 2 \\) is \\( a = 2 \\).\n\nTherefore, the largest constant \\( a \\) such that \\( P(G(a)) \\) is defined is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:1163"}
